WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.830
Here is a famous recursive function

2
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:07.190
the Fibonacci numbers.

3
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.260
Each one is the sum of the two before it.

4
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.680
It takes four lines to write.

5
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.716
Ask it for the thirtieth number

6
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.481
and the computer makes almost

7
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.370
three million calls.

8
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.310
Ask for the fiftieth

9
00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.800
and it makes more than forty billion.

10
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.400
Where do all those calls come from?

11
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.699
To answer that

12
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.538
we need to see what the computer remembers

13
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:30.772
while a recursive function runs

14
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.261
and to draw a picture of

15
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.590
every call it makes.

16
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.100
This is the story of the call stack

17
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.690
and the recursion tree.

18
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.329
In this video we'll see how a stack of

19
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.830
frames remembers unfinished calls

20
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.159
how to draw every call as a tree

21
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.843
and how counting the nodes of

22
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:49.840
that tree tells us the cost.

23
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.382
Start with something smaller

24
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.417
factorial of three is three

25
00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.340
times factorial of two.

26
00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.060
When a function calls itself

27
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.600
the computer cannot forget where it was.

28
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.540
It stores a frame: the arguments

29
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.650
and the place to continue from.

30
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.150
Frames go on a stack.

31
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.090
Factorial of three calls factorial of two

32
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.710
which calls factorial of one

33
00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.690
which calls factorial of zero.

34
00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.000
Four frames, one on top of the other.

35
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.200
Factorial of zero is the base case.

36
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.160
It returns one without calling anyone.

37
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.946
The top frame is popped

38
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.706
and the frame below it receives

39
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.630
its answer and can finish.

40
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:33.370
One, one, two, six.

41
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.530
Last in, first out

42
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.010
exactly the stack from Chapter three.

43
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.740
The deepest call is the first to return.

44
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.350
Here is the same run drawn as a tree.

45
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.083
Each call is a node.

46
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.873
A call that makes another call

47
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.640
has it as its child.

48
00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.300
For factorial the tree is just a chain

49
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.304
n plus one nodes

50
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.419
and n plus one frames on the stack

51
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.070
at the deepest moment.

52
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:01.350
Two numbers describe any recursive function.

53
00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.590
The number of nodes is the time.

54
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.940
The depth of the tree is the stack space.

55
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.230
Fibonacci is different.

56
00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:11.810
Each call makes two calls

57
00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.880
fib of n minus one, and fib of n minus two.

58
00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.040
Here is the tree for fib of five.

59
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.800
Count the nodes: fifteen calls

60
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.590
to compute a number that is just five.

61
00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.290
Look at the repeats.

62
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.010
Fib of three is computed twice.

63
00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.314
Fib of two, three times.

64
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.332
The tree keeps solving the same

65
00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.660
small problems from scratch.

66
00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.940
And notice the depth: only five levels.

67
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.640
The stack stays shallow

68
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.010
but the tree is wide.

69
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.680
How fast does the number of calls grow?

70
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.340
The count for n is one for the call itself

71
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.980
plus the count for n minus one

72
00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.510
plus the count for n minus two.

73
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.030
Work it out.

74
00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.630
Fib of five: fifteen calls.

75
00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.850
Fib of ten: one hundred seventy-seven.

76
00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.750
Fib of twenty: twenty-one thousand

77
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:05.910
eight hundred ninety-one.

78
00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.670
Fib of thirty: two million

79
00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.250
six hundred ninety-two thousand

80
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.460
five hundred thirty-seven.

81
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.003
Every step up in n multiplies the

82
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.560
work by about one point six.

83
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.916
That is exponential growth

84
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.753
a different animal from the quadratic

85
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:23.650
growth we met in Chapter one.

86
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.920
The tree, not the code, tells you the cost.

87
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.050
The fix is to remember.

88
00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:34.230
Before computing fib of k, look in a table.

89
00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:36.450
If the answer is there, use it.

90
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.900
If not, compute it once and store it.

91
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:43.169
Now every subproblem is solved only once.

92
00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.067
The second copy of fib of

93
00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.840
three becomes a lookup

94
00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.890
and most of the tree disappears.

95
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.850
The call count drops to two n minus one

96
00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:56.390
nine calls for five, nineteen for ten

97
00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:57.750
thirty-nine for twenty

98
00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.430
fifty-nine for thirty.

99
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:01.800
Linear instead of exponential.

100
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:04.680
This idea is called memoization.

101
00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.950
It is the seed of dynamic programming.

102
00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.860
There is one more cost

103
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.040
the stack itself is finite.

104
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.639
Every frame takes memory

105
00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.150
and every language limits how

106
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.850
deep a recursion can go.

107
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.130
Ask for a recursion a million levels deep

108
00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.510
and the program does not just get slow.

109
00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.040
It stops with an error.

110
00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.820
So when you design a recursive function

111
00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.060
ask two questions

112
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.260
how many nodes does the tree have

113
00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.430
and how deep does it go?

114
00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.200
To sum up.

115
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.580
Every call gets a frame

116
00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.980
and unfinished calls form a stack.

117
00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.820
Draw the calls as a tree

118
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.580
the node count is the time

119
00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.650
and the depth is the stack space.

120
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.841
Fibonacci shows how an innocent recursion

121
00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.250
can have an exponential tree

122
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.410
and how remembering answers collapses it.

123
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:55.490
Next time: binary search

124
00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:57.173
where every call throws away

125
00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.440
half of the problem.

126
00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:01.574
Now let's write these functions in Java

127
00:05:01.637 --> 00:05:03.089
and watch the numbers from the

128
00:05:03.137 --> 00:05:04.467
tree appear on screen.

129
00:05:05.047 --> 00:05:06.717
This is factorial.

130
00:05:06.717 --> 00:05:11.397
The base case: if n is zero, return one.

131
00:05:11.397 --> 00:05:12.337
Otherwise

132
00:05:12.337 --> 00:05:16.567
return n times factorial of n minus one.

133
00:05:16.917 --> 00:05:18.647
There is no loop.

134
00:05:18.647 --> 00:05:20.070
The method calls itself

135
00:05:20.127 --> 00:05:22.621
and the Java virtual machine keeps one

136
00:05:22.687 --> 00:05:24.790
frame on the thread's stack for every

137
00:05:24.847 --> 00:05:26.357
call that has not returned.

138
00:05:26.707 --> 00:05:29.761
Notice the return type is long, not int.

139
00:05:29.837 --> 00:05:31.335
Twenty factorial already

140
00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.507
needs sixty-two bits.

141
00:05:34.087 --> 00:05:35.570
To see the stack

142
00:05:35.657 --> 00:05:37.288
here is a version that prints its

143
00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:38.957
arguments on the way down

144
00:05:38.957 --> 00:05:41.767
and its results on the way back up.

145
00:05:42.117 --> 00:05:44.727
The indentation is the depth.

146
00:05:44.727 --> 00:05:47.327
Factorial of zero returns first

147
00:05:47.327 --> 00:05:49.647
and factorial of three returns last.

148
00:05:49.647 --> 00:05:51.677
Last in, first out.

149
00:05:52.257 --> 00:05:53.807
Here is Fibonacci.

150
00:05:53.807 --> 00:05:56.220
To count the calls we add one line

151
00:05:56.287 --> 00:05:58.143
a static counter that goes up

152
00:05:58.207 --> 00:06:00.217
every time the method runs.

153
00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:03.537
For n equals five, fifteen calls.

154
00:06:03.537 --> 00:06:06.997
For ten, one hundred seventy-seven.

155
00:06:06.997 --> 00:06:10.232
For twenty, twenty-one thousand eight

156
00:06:10.317 --> 00:06:11.617
hundred ninety-one.

157
00:06:11.617 --> 00:06:15.317
For thirty, over two and a half million.

158
00:06:15.317 --> 00:06:17.297
These are real runs

159
00:06:17.297 --> 00:06:19.047
and they match the tree.

160
00:06:19.627 --> 00:06:21.589
The memoized version passes a

161
00:06:21.657 --> 00:06:23.317
HashMap down the recursion.

162
00:06:23.317 --> 00:06:26.277
If n is already in the map, we use it.

163
00:06:26.277 --> 00:06:29.467
Otherwise we compute it once and store it.

164
00:06:29.817 --> 00:06:32.087
The public method creates the map

165
00:06:32.087 --> 00:06:33.887
so callers never see it.

166
00:06:33.887 --> 00:06:37.607
The counts are nine, nineteen

167
00:06:37.607 --> 00:06:40.657
thirty-nine and fifty-nine: two n minus one.

168
00:06:41.237 --> 00:06:44.367
Here is a trap Python does not have.

169
00:06:44.367 --> 00:06:48.607
A long holds at most about nine quintillion.

170
00:06:48.607 --> 00:06:50.629
Factorial of twenty fits.

171
00:06:50.707 --> 00:06:53.455
Factorial of twenty-one silently wraps

172
00:06:53.527 --> 00:06:56.047
around to a negative number

173
00:06:56.047 --> 00:06:58.197
and Java says nothing.

174
00:06:58.547 --> 00:07:00.707
Use BigInteger when the numbers

175
00:07:00.777 --> 00:07:02.467
can get this big.

176
00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:04.787
Java protects itself with

177
00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.727
a StackOverflowError.

178
00:07:07.077 --> 00:07:09.247
Five hundred levels are fine.

179
00:07:09.247 --> 00:07:12.548
A hundred million levels throw the error.

180
00:07:12.627 --> 00:07:15.038
Where exactly it happens depends on

181
00:07:15.107 --> 00:07:16.638
the size of the thread's stack

182
00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:17.918
which you can change with

183
00:07:17.967 --> 00:07:20.077
the dash X s s option.

184
00:07:20.427 --> 00:07:22.097
Unlike Python

185
00:07:22.097 --> 00:07:24.508
Java has no fixed recursion limit

186
00:07:24.577 --> 00:07:26.750
it is the stack's size in

187
00:07:26.837 --> 00:07:28.577
memory that runs out.

188
00:07:28.577 --> 00:07:31.517
And it is an error, not an exception

189
00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.077
you normally don't catch it

190
00:07:33.077 --> 00:07:34.827
you fix the recursion.

191
00:07:35.407 --> 00:07:36.817
So in Java

192
00:07:36.817 --> 00:07:39.297
one frame per call on the thread's stack

193
00:07:39.297 --> 00:07:41.457
an overflow error when it runs out

194
00:07:41.457 --> 00:07:44.487
and long arithmetic that can silently wrap.

195
00:07:44.837 --> 00:07:46.980
Next: binary search

196
00:07:47.087 --> 00:07:48.900
and a recursion that halves

197
00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:50.197
the problem every time.

